[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
**Level 0 of 4 - Digital Design Brief.**

A concise, high-level summary of a planned initiative, written so management can
reach a Go/No-Go decision without technical detail.

**Keep it short.** Two to three pages for a small solution, more only as size
and complexity demand. A reader should get through it in 15-20 minutes. If a
section is running long, the detail belongs at L1 or below.

The Go/No-Go here is about **whether to proceed to solution design** - not about
which option to build. Present the options; let the next phase choose.

This document is marked `ROOT: True`, so its nodes are exempt from the "not
connected to any parent" statistic. Everything at L1 and below traces up to it.
<<<

[[SECTION]]
UID: L0-CONTEXT
TITLE: Context of the initiative

[[SECTION]]
UID: L0-CTX-CURRENT
TITLE: Current situation

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
TBD - The starting point and the problem or opportunity that motivates this.

- What exists today?
- What problem are we solving, or what opportunity are we capturing?
- What is the business impact of not acting?

Quantify where possible. "62% satisfaction, 20-30 minute waits at peak" carries
more weight with this audience than "students are dissatisfied".
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: L0-CTX-MOTIVATION
TITLE: Motivation for change

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
TBD - Why act now.

- What has changed?
- What triggers this - regulation, market shift, technology opportunity?
- What are the consequences of inaction?

This section answers "why not next year", which is usually the first question
from this audience.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

